Functions in C++
Definition: A block of code that performs a specific task.

Syntax:
returnType functionName(parameters) {
    // body
}

Example:
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
Nuance: If no return value, use void. Always declare before use or use prototypes.

2. Function Overloading
Multiple functions with the same name but different parameters (type, number, or order).

Example:
void print(int i) { cout << "Int: " << i; }
void print(double d) { cout << "Double: " << d; }
void print(string s) { cout << "String: " << s; }

3. Loops & Nesting
for, while, do-while.

Nested loop: Loop inside another loop.

for(int i=0; i<3; i++) {
    for(int j=0; j<3; j++) {
        cout << i << "," << j << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
}

4. Conditional Statements
if, if-else, if-else-if-else

if(score >= 90) grade = 'A';
else if(score >= 75) grade = 'B';
else grade = 'C';

5. STL - Vector
Dynamic array.

Common methods: push_back(), pop_back(), size(), at(), [], begin(), end().

2D vector:

vector<vector<int>> mat(3, vector<int>(4, 0)); // 3x4 zero matrix
mat[0][1] = 5;
6. STL - Set
Stores unique elements in sorted order.

set<int> s = {3,1,4,1,5}; // becomes {1,3,4,5}
s.insert(2);
s.erase(3);
7. STL - Map
Key-value pairs, sorted by key (unique keys).

map<string, int> m;
m["apple"] = 5;
m["banana"] = 2;
for(auto &p : m) cout << p.first << ":" << p.second << endl;

8. Strings in C++
#include <string>

Methods: size(), append(), find(), substr(), [] access.

string s = "Hello";
s[0] = 'h'; // "hello"
9. 2D Arrays (C-style)

Fixed size static array.

int arr[3][2] = {{1,2},{3,4},{5,6}};
arr[1][0] = 99; // changes row2 col1

Nuances & Common Mistakes

Topic                               Nuance
Overloading                         Cannot differ only by return type – must differ in parameters.
Vector vs Array                     Vector knows its size, array (C-style) does not. Prefer vector unless fixed small size.
Set insertion                       If you insert duplicate, it’s ignored (no error).
Map []                              If key not present, m[key] creates default value (0 for int).
2D vector initialization	        vector<vector<int>> v(rows, vector<int>(cols, initial_value));
Loop conditions	                    Off-by-one errors common – test with small values.
String vs char array	            Use std::string over char[] unless specific need.
Nested loops	                    Watch variable names – don’t reuse same loop counter.

4 Easy Practice Questions (Directly Applicable)

Q1. Write a function maxOfTwo(int a, int b) returning the larger number. Test with user input.

Q2. Use a loop to print numbers from 1 to 10, but if the number is divisible by 3, print “Fizz” instead.

Q3. Given vector<string> names = {"Alice", "Bob", "Charlie"}, use a range-based for loop to print each name.

Q4. Create a map<string, int> of 3 fruits and their prices. Write a function that returns the price of a given fruit name.

Example Solutions for Quick Reference

Example 1 (overloading):
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int area(int side) { return side*side; } // square
int area(int l, int b) { return l*b; }   // rectangle
float area(float r) { return 3.14*r*r; } // circle

Example 2 (remove duplicates with set):
vector<int> v = {2,5,2,8,5};
set<int> s(v.begin(), v.end());
vector<int> uniqueVec(s.begin(), s.end());

Example 3 (2D vector):
vector<vector<int>> mat = {{1,2,3},{4,5,6}};
int sum = 0;
for(auto row : mat)
    for(auto val : row)
        sum += val;
cout << "Total: " << sum;

Example 4 (map frequency):
vector<string> words = {"cat","dog","cat","bird","dog","cat"};
map<string,int> freq;
for(string w : words) freq[w]++; // increment count